package formal edition "2026-11-12"

namespace formal:
    primitive situation, symbol, set, pair, sequence, collection

    section "Formulas and what satisfies them":

        A formula means a statement that has a truth value in each situation.
        The truth value of a formula is true or false.
        An atom means a formula that has no part that is a formula.
        A situation satisfies a formula exactly when the formula is true in the situation.
        A conjunction means a formula that is true exactly when each part of the formula is true.
        A disjunction means a formula that is true exactly when at least one part of the formula is true.
        A negation means a formula that is true exactly when the part of the formula is false.
        An implication means a formula that has a premise and a conclusion.
        An implication is false exactly when the premise of the implication is true and the conclusion of the implication is false.

        A formula set means a collection of formulas.
        A formula set is satisfiable exactly when at least one situation satisfies each formula of the formula set.
        A formula set is unsatisfiable exactly when no situation satisfies each formula of the formula set.
        A model means a situation that satisfies each formula of a formula set.

    section "Contracts":

        A contract means a pair of an assumption and a guarantee.
        An assumption means the formula that a contract supposes.
        A guarantee means the formula that a contract requires.
        A situation satisfies a contract exactly when the situation does not satisfy the assumption of the contract or the situation satisfies the guarantee of the contract.
        A contract holds vacuously in a situation exactly when the situation does not satisfy the assumption of the contract.

    section "Proofs":

        An inference rule means a rule that derives a formula from formulas.
        A derivation means a sequence of applications of inference rules.
        A contradiction means a formula that no situation satisfies.
        A proof means a derivation that a proof checker accepts.
        A proof checker means a program that checks each step of a derivation against the inference rules.
        A calculus means a set of inference rules.
        A calculus is sound exactly when each formula set that yields a contradiction in the calculus is unsatisfiable.
        A calculus is refutationally complete exactly when each unsatisfiable formula set yields a contradiction in the calculus.
        A calculus is terminating exactly when each derivation of the calculus ends.
        A calculus is confluent exactly when each order of rule applications reaches the same result.
